{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Minnesota Twins were a team that defied expectations from the moment the calendar turned to spring. Nobody had penciled them in for a postseason berth, and early stumbles — a grand slam surrendered on Opening Day, a damaging home run allowed by closer Brandon Kintzler — seemed to confirm the skeptics. Yet the Twins kept finding ways to win, fueled by a surprising power surge (206 home runs on the season) and timely clutch hits from the likes of Joe Mauer, Jason Castro, and breakout star Eddie Rosario.\n\nJune and July brought genuine turbulence. Kintzler's catastrophic blown save against Seattle — a Mike Zunino two-run homer that erased a late lead — was perhaps the low point of the first half, and a brutal stretch against the Dodgers and Athletics in late July sent the Twins to a 10-15 monthly record. Taylor Rogers was torched twice in quick succession, and the bullpen looked like it might unravel the whole season. At the trade deadline, Kintzler was dealt away, a quiet acknowledgment that changes needed to be made.\n\nThen came August, and the Twins caught fire. A stunning 20-10 month transformed them from bubble team to legitimate wild card contender, with Eddie Rosario's clutch single late in August and a Brian Dozier power display down the stretch carrying the offense. Byron Buxton and Rosario combined for a memorable back-to-back homer sequence in mid-September that captured the electricity surrounding this young, hungry roster. Dozier's three-run blast on September 26 felt like a coronation — the Twins were going to the postseason for the first time since 2010.\n\nThe Wild Card game at Yankee Stadium, however, was a sobering end to the Cinderella story. Ervin Santana could not survive the Yankees' Murderers' Row-lite lineup, surrendering a Didi Gregorius three-run homer and a Brett Gardner solo shot that put the game out of reach early. Brian Dozier and Eddie Rosario gave the sparse Twins crowd brief hope with solo shots, but José Berríos couldn't hold things together in relief, and an Aaron Judge homer sealed Minnesota's fate. The 8-4 loss stung, but it could not erase what had been accomplished — a surprise playoff run that announced the Twins were no longer rebuilding, they were arriving.",
